Abstract
This report presents a deep learning-based sentiment analysis system designed to classify movie reviews as positive or negative. Using the IMDB dataset comprising 50,000 balanced movie reviews, we developed and compared two models: a Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory (Bi-LSTM) network with a self-attention mechanism, and a fine-tuned DistilBERT transformer model. Results demonstrate that DistilBERT achieves 93.41% accuracy and 0.9843 AUC-ROC on the test set, outperforming Bi-LSTM which achieves 89.64% accuracy and 0.9598 AUC-ROC. A web-based interface was also developed for real-time prediction of review sentiment.
1. Introduction
1.1 Background and Motivation
The rapid growth of online content has generated enormous volumes of user-generated text, particularly in the form of product and movie reviews. Automatically classifying the sentiment of these reviews is a fundamental task in Natural Language Processing (NLP) with wide practical applications in recommendation systems, business intelligence, and content moderation.
Traditional machine learning approaches such as Naive Bayes and Support Vector Machines rely on hand-crafted features and struggle to capture contextual meaning. Deep learning models, particularly recurrent neural networks and transformer architectures, have demonstrated superior ability to understand language context and semantics.
1.2 Importance and Relevance
Sentiment analysis is a core building block of modern AI applications. For the entertainment industry, automated review classification can help streaming platforms recommend content, studios gauge audience reception, and critics aggregate opinions at scale. The IMDB dataset is one of the most widely used benchmarks in NLP research.
1.3 Objectives
•	Build an end-to-end sentiment analysis pipeline using deep learning
•	Implement and compare two model architectures: Bi-LSTM and DistilBERT
•	Achieve high classification performance across multiple evaluation metrics
•	Develop a web-based interface for real-time sentiment prediction
•	Evaluate and discuss the trade-offs between model complexity and performance





2. Problem Statement
2.1 Problem Definition
Given a text movie review written in natural language, the goal is to classify it as either positive (label=1) or negative (label=0). This is a binary text classification problem. The input is raw, unstructured text of variable length. The output is a binary label alongside a confidence score.
2.2 Challenges in Existing Solutions
•	Variable-length text: Reviews range from 32 to 13,704 characters, requiring robust sequence handling
•	Contextual negation: Phrases like "not brilliant" reverse sentiment and confuse simple keyword-based models
•	Sarcasm and irony: Some reviews use positive language to convey negative sentiment
•	HTML noise: The raw IMDB dataset contains HTML tags such as <br /> that must be cleaned
•	Computational cost: Transformer models require significant memory and compute resources
3. Project Architecture
3.1 System Overview
The system follows a modular pipeline architecture with five stages: data ingestion, preprocessing, model training, evaluation, and inference. Both models share the preprocessing stage but diverge at tokenization and model architecture.
3.2 Pipeline Stages
•	Stage 1 — Data Ingestion: Load the IMDB CSV dataset, validate columns and encode labels.
•	Stage 2 — Text Preprocessing: Strip HTML tags, remove special characters, convert to lowercase.
•	Stage 3 — Tokenization: Custom vocabulary for Bi-LSTM; HuggingFace WordPiece tokenizer for DistilBERT.
•	Stage 4 — Model Training: Train using AdamW optimizer with Cosine Annealing and gradient clipping.
•	Stage 5 — Evaluation: Compute all metrics and generate confusion matrices and ROC curves.
3.3 Model Architectures
Bi-LSTM Model: The input embedding layer maps each word index to a 100-dimensional vector. A two-layer Bidirectional LSTM with 256 hidden units per direction processes the sequence. A self-attention mechanism computes a weighted sum over all hidden states. Two fully connected layers with ReLU activation and dropout (0.4) produce the binary output.
DistilBERT Model: The pre-trained DistilBERT-base-uncased model is loaded with its 6 transformer layers and 768-dimensional hidden states. The [CLS] token representation is extracted, passed through a pre-classifier linear layer with ReLU, dropout (0.3), and a final linear classification layer.
