6.2 Key Findings
•	DistilBERT outperforms Bi-LSTM on all five evaluation metrics, demonstrating the advantage of pre-trained transformers.
•	Bi-LSTM achieves competitive performance (89.64% accuracy) while being significantly faster to train.
•	Both models maintain high AUC-ROC scores above 0.95, indicating strong discriminative power.
•	DistilBERT shows strongest improvement in precision (+4.19%), reducing false positive predictions.
6.3 Practical Applications
•	Movie streaming platforms can automatically tag and filter user reviews
•	Film studios can monitor audience reception in real-time during release periods
•	The web interface enables non-technical users to analyze reviews without coding knowledge


7. Discussion
7.1 Model Comparison
DistilBERT's pre-trained contextual representations provide a significant advantage over Bi-LSTM's learned embeddings. DistilBERT understands complex linguistic patterns including negation, sarcasm, and domain-specific vocabulary. However, it requires approximately 4x longer training time and more memory.
The Bi-LSTM with attention is a strong lightweight alternative. Its self-attention layer effectively identifies sentiment-bearing words, making it interpretable and performant. For resource-constrained environments, Bi-LSTM offers a practical trade-off.
7.2 Challenges and Mitigations
•	Long reviews: Truncating to 256 tokens may discard useful information. Mitigation: Use hierarchical attention for very long texts.
•	Sarcasm detection: Both models occasionally misclassify sarcastic reviews. Mitigation: Augment training data with sarcastic examples.
•	Training time: DistilBERT takes 1-2 hours on CPU. Mitigation: Use a GPU with CUDA support.
•	Domain shift: Models trained on IMDB may not generalize to other domains. Mitigation: Fine-tune on domain-specific data.
